// secret_reveal_screen.dart
//
// Per-player secret reveal. Each player swipes up the covering card to peek
// at their word or hint, then passes the device to the next player.
//
// Card mechanics
// --------------
// A card covers the secret. Dragging it up reveals the content underneath;
// releasing it slides the card back down. The reveal flag is set the first
// time the card drops below a threshold value, so a single peek registers
// per player and repeated drags during the same peek do not re-fire haptics.
//
// After a fixed period of inactivity before any drag, a looping Lottie
// animation appears at the bottom of the card to teach the swipe gesture.
// The animation is disarmed as soon as a drag starts.
//
// Role-aware content
// ------------------
// Civilians always see their word. Imposters see their hint if the hint
// option is enabled; otherwise they see only the role label. In hidden
// imposter mode, the imposter is shown a hint styled as if it were a
// civilian word so they do not realise they are the imposter.
//
// Session-not-found fallback
// --------------------------
// A null session is not a loading state — it means "no game in progress".
// Reaching this screen without a session (deep link, restored state, a
// misrouted navigation) shows an explicit error screen with a path back to
// Home rather than a spinner that would never resolve.
//
// Reveal-to-ready transition
// --------------------------
// When the last player has been revealed, the session's current player
// becomes null and this screen would render nothing for one frame while
// the Ready screen mounts. To avoid a visible flash, the fallback case
// renders the same visual as the previous frame — the last player's content
// — and navigates to Ready in a post-frame callback. Navigation is
// deliberately absent from `_advance` so that the route is replaced exactly
// once, not twice.
//
// Layout
// ------
// The card area uses `ClipRRect` inside `Expanded`, so image and background
// paints are clipped rather than overflowing. The bottom pass section pins
// its primary button below a scrollable label area, so the button remains
// reachable on short screens or at large text scales. Every `Text` in this
// file uses `maxLines` + `overflow`, and the reveal content is wrapped in a
// scroll view so a tall word or an extreme text scale scrolls rather than
// throwing a layout overflow.
//
// Strings in this file are currently hardcoded English constants declared
// near the top. The error screen uses `AppLocalizations`; the rest are
// candidates for localization but are deliberately extracted here so the
// swap is a single-line edit per string when ARB keys are added.

